 you need to form three more melds and a pair. Current hand tiles: W1,W1, W7, T2,T3,T4, B4,B4, B7,B7, J2.  \n  If you form Chow [T1,T2,T3], then leftover: T4, W1,W1, W7, B4,B4, B7,B7, J2. That's 9 tiles, need to form two more melds and a pair. Possible: B4,B4,B4 pung? Need one more B4 (unshown B4:2 left). B7,B7,B7 pung? Need one more B7 (unshown B7:3 left). Then pair from W1 or J2. So draws: T1 (4), B4 (2), B7 (3) \u2013 multiple possibilities.  \n  Alternatively, if you draw a tile that makes a pair and then you have two pairs left, you need to complete another meld.  \n  Better to keep flexibility.  \n\nBut note: the valid actions are only discards because it's your turn and you are not winning yet. You must discard one tile.  \nWhich discard improves your hand's potential?  \n- Discarding J2: Removes a single honor, increases chance for all simples? But you have W1 terminal anyway. J2 doesn't contribute to chows.  \n- Discarding W1: Removes a terminal pair, but you have other pairs. W1 is terminal, may limit patterns.  \n- Discarding T2,T3,T4: Breaks the possible Chow, but if T1 is the only possible, keeping them gives 4 outs.  \n- Discarding B4 or B7: Breaks a pair, reducing pung chances.  \n\nGiven the depleted tiles (T5, W6, B1, B8, J3), your hand has many live tiles: B4 (2 left), B7 (3 left), T1 (4 left), W7 (likely 1-2 left), J2 (3 left).  \nBest discard is probably the one that doesn't reduce live draws and maybe improves hand shape.  \nDiscard W7: It's a single, no direct connection. Keeping it only gives 1-2 outs to pair. Discarding it retains all pairs and the T2-3-4 sequence.  \nDiscard J2: Similarly, single honor.  \nBetween W7 and J2, which is more useless? J2 is honor, can form Dragon Pung if you get two more, but unlikely. W7 is suited, could pair or form Chow with W5,W6,W8,W9, but W6 gone, W5,W8,W9 available. Actually W7 can form Chow [W5,W6,W7] (W6 gone), [W6,W7,W8] (W6 gone), [W7,W8,W9] (possible, W8:2, W9:1 left). So W7 has Chow potential with W8,W9 (needs W8 and W9, but you have only one W7). Actually to form a Chow with W7, you need two specific tiles, which is less likely than pairing. So W7's best use is pairing.  \nComparing live draws for pairing: W7: ~2 left?; J2: 3 left. So J2 has more pairing outs.  \nThus discard W7 might be slightly better than discard J2.  \n\nBut also consider discarding T4: Then you have T2,T3 which can form Chow with T1 or T4 (but T4 discarded) or T5 (gone). So only T1 left, that's 4 outs. But if you keep T2,T3,T4, you have same 4 outs to T1. So keeping all three doesn't increase outs, but it does allow the Chow to be completed with one draw. If you discard one, you'd need two draws to complete a Chow (e.g., need T1 and T4 or T1 and T5). So keeping the three-tile sequence is valuable.  \n\nTherefore, discard either W7 or J2.  \nGiven J2 is honor and might be needed for some patterns (but unlikely), and W7 is simple, maybe discard W7 to move towards All Simples? But you have W1 terminal, so All Simples not possible.  \nMaybe discard J2 to reduce honor count?  \n\nBut look at patterns: if you win with hand including J2 pair, you get Dragon Pung? No, pung requires three. Pair doesn't count.  \nSo both are singles.  \n\nI think discard W7 is fine.  \n\nReason: W7 is a lone tile with limited connectivity (W6 depleted, Chow chances low). J2 has more live tiles for pairing (3 left vs likely 1-2 for W7). Keeping J2 retains possibility of drawing two more for Dragon Pung (though unlikely). Also, discarding W7 avoids potential conflict with across player's Chow [W7,W8,W9] \u2013 they already have W7, so maybe less chance others need W7? But not major.  \n\nFinal choice: Play W7.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
