B9:2). Also Winds/Dragons: F1:2 left, J2:1 left, F3:2 left, F4:1 left.  \\nMy hand currently is close to \\\"Half Flush\\\" (honors + one suit) because I have W1 pung (honor? W1 is Character-1, not honor, so not honor; actually W1 is suited tile terminal, not wind). Wait, F1 is East Wind. So my melds include T2,T3,T4 (Bamboo), B1,B2,B3 (Dots), W1,W1,W1 (Character). So already three suits present, so Half Flush is impossible.  \\n\\nScoring potential so far:  \\n- Pung of W1 (terminal) = 1 point Pung of Terminals or Honors.  \\n- Prevalent wind (East) is F1, not W1, so no Prevalent wind from W1 pung. Actually W1 is Character-1 terminal, not wind tile. So Prevalent Wind is F1; my Pung is W1, not wind, so no Prevalent Wind points.  \\n- Seat wind same (F1), also not applicable.  \\nSo only 1 point from Pung of Terminals so far.  \\nChow B1,B2,B3: gives Two Terminal Chows (1 point) because it's 123 in Dots.  \\nChow T2,T3,T4: no special pattern yet unless with another Chow in Bamboo suit for Short Straight.  \\n\\nTotal so far: 2 points minimum, need \\u22658 to win.  \\n\\nI need to form one more meld and a pair. Possible directions:  \\n1. Try for \\\"All Chows\\\" (2 points) + \\\"All Simples\\\" (2 points) if I avoid terminals/honors in new meld/pair.  \\n2. Try for \\\"Mixed Triple Chow\\\" if I can get three same-number chows in different suits. Currently have Chow 2,3,4 in Bamboo; Chow 1,2,3 in Dots; could try for Chow 1,2,3 in Characters too, but W1 is used in pung, so maybe not.  \\n3. Try for \\\"Mixed Straight\\\" (8 points) if I get chows 123, 456, 789 in three different suits. Currently: B1,B2,B3 (Dots 123); T2,T3,T4 (Bamboo 234); need 456 in Characters? But W1 pung uses W1, not helpful. Actually Mixed Straight needs 123 in one suit, 456 in another, 789 in third. I have 123 in Dots, but 456 must be in different suit, 789 in third. I have T2,T3,T4 (Bamboo 234), not 456. Could change T2,T3,T4 into T4,T5,T6 by discarding T2 or T3? But it's fixed as exposed meld.  \\nSo Mixed Straight impossible now.  \\n\\nCheck \\\"All Types\\\" (6 points): requires all three suits + wind + dragon. I have three suits already in melds, so need wind pung/chow and dragon pung/chow. I have none yet. Could get F1 pung if draw two more F1 (2 left) and use one from hand? Hand has no winds. Difficult.  \\n\\nCheck \\\"Outside Hand\\\" (4 points): every meld and pair must contain terminals or honors. My current melds: B1,B2,B3 has terminal B1; T2,T3,T4 no terminal/honor; W1 pung has terminal W1. So one meld lacks terminal/honor. So Outside Hand not possible unless I change T2,T3,T4 chow into terminal chow, but exposed meld fixed. So Outside Hand not possible.  \\n\\nGiven hand: W8 (terminal), B4x2 (simple), B7x2 (simple).  \\nIf I keep B4x2 and B7x2, I can try to complete a pair from either and get a Chow from the other with a draw.  \\nPossible chow: B4,B5,B6 needs B5 and B6 (2 each left) or B2,B3,B4 needs B2 (3 left) but B3 is 0 left; B6,B7,B8 needs B8 (0 left); B5,B6,B7 needs B5,B6 (2 each).  \\nOr pung: B4 pung needs one more B4 (2 left), B7 pung needs one more B7 (3 left).  \\n\\nBut to win I must have a legal hand (4 melds+pair). Currently 3 melds + 3 tiles in hand = need one meld (3 tiles) and a pair (2 tiles) but total 5 tiles needed, I have only 3 tiles, so I must either:  \\n- Convert one tile into a pair and the other two into part of a meld, needing one more tile to complete meld, OR  \\n- Use two tiles as part of a pair and one tile as part of meld, needing two more tiles.  \\n\\nActually, I have 3 melds exposed (9 tiles) + 3 hand tiles = 12 tiles. To win I need 14 tiles, so I need 2 more tiles (by drawing or claiming). So I can't complete hand now; must discard.  \\n\\nGoal: Discard safest tile while keeping flexible for high-scoring pattern.  \\n\\nTiles in hand:  \\n- W8: terminal, only 2 left unseen, relatively safe because many W8 gone? History shows W8 played by next player and across player, so maybe 2 left.  \\n- B4: simple, 2 left unseen, B4 melded by previous player as Chow B4,B5,B6, so B4 not too safe (they may need B4 for pung).  \\n- B7: simple, 3 left unseen, seems safer.  \\n\\nAlso consider opponents' discards:  \\nPrevious player discarded B7, W7, etc. B7 maybe safe.  \\n\\nEvaluation of possible discards:  \\n1. Play B7: keeps W8 terminal (can pair with W8 if draw another) and B4x2 (potential B4 pung or Chow B4,B5,B6). B7 is simple, less likely to give others pung.  \\n2. Play W8: discarding terminal may be safe if others don't need terminals; keeps B4x2 and B7x2, better chance for pung or chow in Dots.  \\n3. Play B4: discarding a tile that previous player used in chow may be risky if they need B4 for pung; but B4 is simple.  \\n\\nBetter to keep duplicates (B4x2, B7x2) because they can form pair or pung. Discard W8 singleton terminal.  \\n\\n**Reason:**  \\nDiscarding W8 reduces terminal clutter, keeps two potential pairs (B4, B7) for flexibility. B4 and B7 both have several unseen tiles left to complete pung or chow. W8 is terminal but only 2 left, not highly dangerous. Keeping B4x2 and B7x2 maximizes chances to complete a meld+pair with fewer draws. Also, W8 discard is less likely to feed pung/chow to opponents based on history (W8 already used in meld by across player, less demand).  \\n\\n**Answer:** Play W8\"}"}
