ility.  \n\nBut I must also consider scoring patterns. With exposed melds: B1-B2-B3 Chow, T2-T3-T4 Chow, W1 Pung. These include terminals (B1, T2? T2 is simple? T2 is bamboo 2, not terminal). B1 is terminal. W1 is terminal. So I have terminal tiles in melds. Possible patterns: All Types (since I have three suits and winds? I have W1 wind? W1 is character 1, not wind. Wind tiles: I have none in melds. Dragons: none. So not yet All Types. But I could aim for Half Flush? Not likely.  \n\nGiven I'm close to winning, I should discard the tile with lowest chance to complete a meld. T1 and T4 both have poor Chow prospects due to missing adjacent tiles. W4 has better prospects with W3 available.  \n\nReason:  \nDiscarding T4 seems best because T4's adjacent tiles (T3, T5) are mostly gone (T3 has 2 left but T5 gone, so cannot form a Chow). T1's adjacent tiles (T2, T3) are also problematic (T2 gone). W4 has W3 abundant and W5 scarce but possible. Also, keeping B4 pair is essential for the pair in winning hand. So discard T4 to keep W4 and T1, hoping to draw W3 or W5 to complete a Chow with W4, or draw another B4 for a Pung, or draw a tile that pairs with T1 or W4 to then seek a meld.  \n\nAnswer: Play T4"}
